  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "What surface is defined by the equation $\u03d5=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in spherical polar coordinates?",
  "worked_solution": "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,\u03d5),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\u03d5\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\u03d5=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis must equal $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-cone.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/27f9b580436d8e1aebeb4f150643a22266e9ad7f488b98e63f8a49dd4f1aa97f.png)",
